    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-74532",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b010a518-7aa3-5b53-9a6d-ef0779ff0dfa",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Reduced Row Echelon Form of a Singular 3x3 System With Two Pivots",
      "problem": "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-y+z=1 \\\\ x-y+z=-1 \\\\ 2y-2z=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system.",
      "worked_solution": "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ into reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the row echelon form above, we have only two pivots (in the $1$st and $2$nd columns). To transform the matrix to reduced row echelon form, we need to create zeros above the pivots only. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ y-z=3 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation, we can remove this equation from the system. \n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ y=3+z \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3+z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3+z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "No solution"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3+z \\\\ 1-z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"
            }
          ],
          "correct_value": "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3+z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )",
          "choices_complete": true,
