      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Reduced Row Echelon Form of a 3x3 System That Is Not in Row Echelon Form",
      "problem": "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079061/assets/3e66da254c5da8b28c86908dbe0d0100489db7ed9f1c752b2e253160b603ac45.png)\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system.",
      "worked_solution": "The augmented matrix for this system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079061/assets/1851c6530047d54eb28a337292ad4466caecade797c2cba39808eb85daf6e3e8.png)\n\nTransforming $M$ into reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079061/assets/f21067ccbed84ad8a3d52edb479d613f9291a0a51f34a4237ff7a42d25a1eb93.png)\n\nSo, we have the solution $x=3,$ $y=-2,$ and $z=0.$ Or, in vector form, $\\langle 3,-2,0 \\rangle.$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-6233",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b010a518-7aa3-5b53-9a6d-ef0779ff0dfa",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Reduced Row Echelon Form of a Singular 3x3 System With Two Pivots",
      "problem": "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-2y=2 \\\\ x-4z=6 \\\\ -3x+6y=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system.",
      "worked_solution": "The augmented matrix for this system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079061/assets/89b1f5f2dbc7e2677b0f73445601a1c7280f218bd50b7e8607c9b2cb9d2d5179.png)\n\nTransforming $M$ into reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the row echelon form above, we have only two pivots (in the $1$st and $2$nd columns). To transform the matrix to reduced row echelon form, we need to create zeros above the pivots only. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079061/assets/ed183109cb48a15a2f1f2309b8d5e16525d781e37db1ebc8c3c528720e90e808.png)\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation, we can remove this equation from the system. \n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=4z+6 \\\\ y=2z+2 \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4z+6 \\\\ 2z+2 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-6234",
      "knowledge_point_id": "aa91091b-5368-5d80-b87d-08b3524177f3",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Reduced Row Echelon Form of a Singular 3x3 System With One Pivot",
      "problem": "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-6y+2z=8 \\\\ -6x+18y-6z=-24 \\\\ 4x-12y+4z=16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form.",
      "worked_solution": "The augmented matrix for this system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079061/assets/90fe26cf9a7faf4027ee6544f7d13572559edfd90a0323800dc81ec9ef6a2f14.png)\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 & 2 & 8 \\\\ -6 & 18 & -6 & -24 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 4 & 16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 4 \\\\ -6 & 18 & -6 & -24 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 4 & 16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 4 & 16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079061/assets/81c5226eb70fe91ccac01de8b8539dec7eb792765a23d38ff2fa2dd1a6e4d8c9.png)",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    }
  ]
}
